{
  "$schema": "../../schema/incident.schema.json",
  "protocol": "Biswap",
  "name": "Biswap V3Migrator migrate() spends any approver's V2 LP, with unchecked pair and tokens",
  "summary": "Biswap's V3Migrator.migrate(params) pulls params.liquidityToMigrate of params.pair LP from params.recipient rather than from the caller, burns it, mints a V3 position in params.token0/params.token1 for the recipient and refunds the unused amounts, without checking that the tokens are the pair's or that the pair is genuine, so anyone could spend the LP of every holder who had approved the migrator. The first deployment 0xb7933eb7 sent the refund to msg.sender: in the anchor, generalized front-runner 0xf576c7f1 (contract 0x25f1424b) copied 0xa8265823's pending migration, whose own transaction then reverted, and kept the 9.41 USDT refund, and bots front-ran 75 migrations this way within a day. The same deployer redeployed the migrator as 0x839b0afd with refunds to the recipient, but the pull from the recipient and the unchecked tokens remained. From block 29552670 attackers migrated victims' LP into two fake tokens, which left the real underlying in the migrator, and took it with a second migrate naming their own contract as the 'pair' (the first took 121,855.90 BSW and 51.32 WBNB of 0x1bc2bb87's 2,187.77 BSW/WBNB LP). Three attackers and four smaller copycats did this in 91 transactions on the redeploy, the last in May 2024 (block 38761669), and 0x740d618e in three on the first deployment.",
